# Bento Grid

Use modular, differently sized content cells as a primary preset while keeping the grid available as an independent layout capability.

- **Palette:** Light background `#F4F7FB`, foreground `#172033`, primary `#4F46E5`, accent `#F97316`, surface `#FFFFFF`, border `#D8E0EC`, focus `#3730A3`. Dark background `#0B1020`, foreground `#F8FAFC`, surface `#172036`, primary `#A5B4FC`, accent `#FDBA74`.
- **Type:** Modern sans with compact card titles, readable summaries, and tabular metric numerals. Type scale stays consistent across cell sizes.
- **Surfaces:** 16–24px rounded tiles, subtle separation, consistent internal padding, and occasional tonal feature cells. Cell importance comes from span and content, not arbitrary decoration.
- **Components:** Compose shared cards, charts, actions, forms, and status lists inside CSS Grid. Every tile needs a meaningful heading; do not create a separate component copy per span.
- **Interaction:** Hover raises actionable tiles only; static tiles do not pretend to be buttons. Keyboard focus follows DOM reading order, not visual placement. Disabled actions remain labeled and visually distinct from static tiles.
- **Responsive:** Use named spans at wide widths, a simpler two-column tablet grid, and one column on small screens. Do not use dense masonry that reorders content.
- **Accessibility:** DOM order must remain logical at every breakpoint. Give charts summaries, preserve labels, and avoid making a whole complex card one ambiguous link.
- **Theme/performance:** Both modes have distinct cell boundaries. Use CSS Grid without layout libraries; size media to prevent shifts and avoid animating grid geometry under reduced motion.

Representative preview: SaaS overview with one wide summary, metric cells, activity list, compact form, and highlighted next action.
